# Git commit messages

When creating a git commit, write the message in this shape:

```text
type: short summary in imperative mood

- Most important change
- Next important change
- Optional third change
```

## Title

- Use [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/): `type: summary`
- Common types: `feat`, `fix`, `refact`, `docs`, `chore`, `build`, `test`
- Imperative mood ("disable X", not "disabled X" / "disables X")
- ~72 characters or less; no trailing period
- Focus on **why** / the outcome, not a file list

## Body (bullet points)

- Always add a blank line after the title
- Use `-` bullets for the most important changes (usually 2–5)
- Each bullet is one short, concrete change
- Prefer user/dev-facing behavior over implementation trivia
- Skip bullets only for trivial one-line commits where the title is enough

## Examples

```text
fix: keep serial streaming when object tab loses focus

- Stop closing the COM port on tab deactivate
- Match baud to SERIAL_BAUDRATE so firmware stays in sync
```

```text
feat: map six pin slots through OSC and serial frames

- Align Pin_A…F order with firmware ingest
- Send inactive slots as WIRE_INACTIVE_PIN_SENTINEL
```

## Do not

- Do not write a long prose paragraph body
- Do not dump every touched file as a bullet
- Do not use vague bullets like "update code" or "fix stuff"
